# ============================================================
#  Baseline pools, second pass (2026-10-24): castle_more.
#  Repeatable routine events for the castle places that had the
#  thinnest pools: library, lecture tower, great hall, infirmary,
#  alchemy laboratory, Mir's workshop, the Ledger office, the
#  headmistress's tower, the seventh tower and the thesis lab.
#  Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.
# ============================================================

# ---------------- The Library ----------------

@event library_whispering_dome
title: The Whispering Dome
pool: library_routine
tone: light
art: book
tags: study
scene: A gallery railing under a painted dome at the top of the library, a student leaning over it with one ear cocked, seven floors of shelves below.
text: {Under the dome on the sixth floor the echoes are wrong in a useful way. A whisper anywhere in the library arrives here, clear as a bell.|The dome is carrying voices again. Someone has chalked a small ear on the railing.|You know the dome's geography now: by the pillar you hear the front desk; by the window, the stacks.} {Two voices below are discussing a test that hasn't been set yet.|Someone below is rehearsing a proposal of marriage, badly.|{season == "winter": The radiators hiss over half of it.|A prefect is reading out demerits to himself, with relish.}}
> left: Lean on the rail and listen
  guile +small
  alignment -tiny
  say: {You learn the third question of next week's test, probably. Eavesdroppers, it turns out, don't feel as clever as they'd hoped.|The rehearsal ends with a yes, said in the same voice as the question. You feel like a thief who has stolen a wedding that hasn't happened yet.|You learn exactly what the front desk thinks of you. It's fair, which is worse.}
> right: Cough loudly, and read
  lore +small
  say: {Your cough rolls down the dome like thunder. Seven floors stop whispering at once, and you read in a silence you made.|You cough. Somewhere below, the proposal stops mid-sentence. You read a whole chapter before you stop wondering how it ended.|You cough on purpose now. Below, every whisper stops, and the dome is yours, and so is the chapter.}

@event library_after_closing
slot: evening
title: After the Closing Bell
pool: library_routine
tone: eerie
art: lantern
tags: study
scene: A library at closing time, lamps going out floor by floor from the top down, a student half-hidden between two shelves with a book held to their chest.
text: {The bell rings to close the upper galleries, and their lamps begin to go out from the top floor down, one floor a minute. Students drift down to the reading room, which keeps its lamps for the night.|The galleries' closing bell. You've noticed the shelves up here lean together a little after hours, as if to talk.|The bell, the lamps, the drift down the stairs. You know the alcove on the fourth floor where the last lamp dies.} {Footnote is doing his rounds, tail up.|Rain is loud on the high windows.|{final_year: You wonder who will hide in your alcove next year.|Somewhere a book closes itself with a snap.}}
> left: Stay hidden past closing
  challenge deceive apprentice:
    win:
      lore +small
      health -tiny
      say: {You read by a stolen candle until the small hours. The library is a different animal at night, and it lets you be.|The shelves lean together and whisper while you read. You catch one word in three, and none of them are kind about the history section.|You read in your alcove till the candle drowns. Walking back across the bridges at two, you feel like a burglar who stole only sentences.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      renown -tiny
      say: {A shelf shuffles sideways and nudges you, firmly, toward the stairs. A prefect on the stair watches you evicted by furniture.|The shelves close the aisle behind you like a mouth and walk you down to the reading room. The story is round the house by breakfast.|Footnote finds you, sits on your book and yowls until the porter comes. The porter tells everyone. Footnote is unrepentant.}
> right: Leave with the crowd
  health +small
  say: {You go to bed at a decent hour, which feels almost illicit.|You leave with everyone else and sleep like a shelved book.|You walk out under the dying lamps and sleep well. The alcove can wait for you.}

# ---------------- The Lecture Tower ----------------

@event lecture_tower_faculty_basket
title: The Faculty Basket
pool: lecture_tower_routine
tone: light
art: tower
tags: class
when: term
scene: A wicker basket on a rope rising up the hollow core of a spiral tower, a sign on it reading FACULTY ONLY, a student eyeing it from the foot of the stairs.
text: {Up the hollow middle of the Lecture Tower runs a wicker basket on a rope, for faculty knees. A sign says FACULTY ONLY. It's two hundred and twelve steps to the top.|The basket is waiting at the bottom again. Someone has added "AND THEIR LUGGAGE" to the sign.|The basket. You know its creak, its sway, and the exact landing where it likes to stop.} {Professor Thorne's satchel is riding up in it, alone.|It still smells faintly of Professor Vey's perfume.|{season == "summer": The stairwell is an oven by the fifth landing.|A draught howls up the stairwell like a chimney.}}
> left: Ride it up, quietly
  challenge deceive apprentice wild:
    win:
      mood +small
      health +tiny
      say: {You ride up beside the satchel, which seems to disapprove, and step off at the top fresh as morning.|You glide past six landings and step out at the top as if you'd walked. Your knees send their thanks.|The basket stops where it likes to stop; you know the trick of the rope now, and arrive at the top unwinded and unseen.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {The satchel's owner is waiting at the top. Thorne looks at you, then at the sign. "Hm." You walk back down, then up.|The basket stops between floors. Professor Vey leans over the rail above. "Faculty," she says, "are generally taller." It's ten minutes before anyone winds you down.|It sticks at its favourite landing, and a whole lecture hall files past, one by one, to look at you.}
> right: Climb all two hundred steps
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You arrive at the top red-faced and righteous, legs like wet bread.|You count the steps aloud. Somewhere beside you the basket creaks past, empty, smug.|You take the steps two at a time now, and only stop to breathe at the landing where the basket stops.}

@event lecture_tower_office_hours
title: The Office-Hours Queue
pool: lecture_tower_routine
tone: warm
art: scroll
tags: study class
cast:
  prof: present(place) where tagged(prof, teacher) and met(prof)
scene: A queue of students on a stone landing outside a half-open office door, one nervous first-year clutching an essay at the very back.
text: {{prof} keeps office hours on the landing, and the queue already reaches the stairs.|Office hours. {prof}'s queue has its etiquette now: no pushing, no crying, no essays read aloud.|The office-hours queue. You know the landing's cold step and the warm one.} At the very back a first-year is holding an essay like a shield{season == "winter": , breath fogging|}.
> left: Wait your turn and ask
  lore +small
  mood -small
  if npc_is(thorne):
    say: {Forty minutes on the stair for four minutes of Thorne. "Hm. Wrong, but interestingly." You'll take it.|Thorne reads your question, crosses out half of it, and answers the half that's left. It was the half that mattered.|Thorne lets you in, answers you in three sentences and says "Next." You carry the three sentences around for a week.}
  elif npc_is(vey):
    say: {Vey answers your question with a question, and then another, until you answer it yourself. It takes an hour you didn't have.|Vey asks how you think you did. You lie. She smiles and tells you exactly how you did.|Vey listens, then asks what you actually came to ask. You hadn't known, until then.}
  elif npc_is(marisol):
    say: {Marisol answers with a poem about rivers, forgets the last line, and somehow the forgetting answers your question.|Marisol talks you through it while watering a fern on the sill. It takes an hour. It's a very good hour.|Marisol hears you out, then opens the window to let the weather in. "There. Now ask me again."}
  else:
    say: {The wait is long, the answer short, and worth it.|You reach the door just before it shuts and get your answer standing on the threshold.|You get your answer, and a reading list longer than the queue.}
> right: Give the first-year your place
  alignment +tiny
  mood +small
  say: {The first-year goes in white and comes out pink and grateful. You never do get to ask your question.|You swap places. The first-year comes out an hour later having been told their essay "has a pulse". They take it as a triumph. So do you.|You give up your place again. The first-year waves the essay at you on the way out, like a flag.}

# ---------------- The Great Hall ----------------

@event great_hall_house_banners
title: The House Banners
pool: great_hall_routine
tone: light
art: crown
tags: social
scene: Five long house banners hanging over the tables of an empty great hall, one of them conspicuously wrong.
text: {The five house banners over the long tables move with their houses' moods. Today Duskgate's hangs upside down, and is furious about it.|Someone has been at the banners again: Brasswick's has been embroidered with a small, elegant moustache.|Another banner prank: Rootward's has been swapped for a tablecloth with a turnip painted on it. The real one is sulking in a corner.} {The tables are empty; supper is an hour off.|A ladder leans against the wall, still warm from the culprit.|{season == "winter": The banners shiver in the draught from the high windows.|Emberhall's banner is smouldering slightly, in sympathy or envy.}}
> left: Put it right before supper
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: {Twenty minutes up a wobbling ladder. Duskgate's banner, righted, gives a grudging flap. Your palms are rope-burned.|You unpick the moustache stitch by stitch from the top of the ladder. Brasswick's banner shudders with relief; your neck doesn't.|You haul the real banner up and the tablecloth down. Rootward's banner settles over its table like a hen on eggs. Your arms ache till morning.}
> right: Improve the prank
  challenge deceive apprentice:
    win:
      guile +small
      mood +small
      say: {You add a matching upside-down candle to the table below. At supper Duskgate laughs hardest, which is how you know it was them.|You give the moustache a monocle. Brasswick declares it an improvement and votes to keep it.|You paint a crown on the turnip. Rootward is outraged, then proud, then outraged again.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {A prefect finds you on the ladder with an upside-down candle, and the whole prank is pinned on you by supper.|You're halfway through a monocle when Brasswick's entire table walks in. You are now the moustache person.|You're caught with paint on your hands and a crown half done. Rootward sends a strongly worded turnip to your table.}

@event great_hall_stagehands
title: Stagehands Wanted
place: great_hall
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: music
tags: festival social
scene: Students on ladders painting a huge backdrop on the great hall's stage while a cook carries in a tray of tasting plates.
text: {season == "autumn": Scenery for the Harvest Feast is going up on the stage: a painted moon the size of a barn.|{season == "winter": They're building the Midwinter Ball's ice arch out of painted canvas and optimism.|{season == "spring": The Spring Tournament's banners are being hemmed on the stage, in five house colours.|They're painting the Starfall backdrop, a whole sky, star by star.}}} {A sign by the stage says STAGEHANDS WANTED, NO EXPERIENCE NECESSARY.|The sign now says SOME EXPERIENCE PREFERRED. Nobody is checking.|Someone hands you a brush as you come in, just in case.} Meanwhile the kitchens are testing the feast menu on anyone who sits still.
> left: Paint, hammer and hold ladders
  arcana +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You paint for three hours and charm the paint to shimmer at the edges. It's the best thing on the stage. Your shoulders disagree.|You hold ladders, hammer, paint, hit your thumb twice and get paint in your hair that won't come out till the festival. Someone stands back and says "oh", the good kind.|They give you the tricky bit, the part that has to move when the candles do, because nobody else will. It does. You get splinters, and applause.}
> right: Taste-test for the kitchens
  health +small
  mood +small
  study -tiny
  say: {You eat seven tasting plates and give honest marks. The pear tart wins. Your revision notes stay shut.|You taste and judge and taste again. The cook writes down everything you say, and your afternoon of reading is gone.|The cook saves you the corner seat and the first spoonful. The menu is better for you; your reading pile is taller.}

# ---------------- The Infirmary ----------------

@event infirmary_night_watch
slot: evening
title: Turning the Fever Charm
pool: infirmary_routine
tone: warm
art: candle
tags: social
scene: A dim infirmary ward at night, one lamp lit by a bed where a sleeping student lies under a slowly turning ring of pale light.
text: {A first-year in the end bed has a fever, and the cooling charm over the bed has to be turned by hand every hour until dawn. Sister Amaranth looks at you, then at the chair.|Another fever, another chair. The cooling charm's brass ring is worn smooth where hands turn it.|The chair by the end bed. Sister Amaranth has left a cushion on it, which is as close as she comes to asking.} {The ward is quiet except for the clock.|Rain is drumming on the infirmary roof.|{season == "winter": Frost feathers the window behind the bed.|Moths keep the lamp company.}}
> left: Take the night watch
  alchemy +small
  health -small
  alignment +tiny
  say: {You turn the ring every hour and watch the fever ebb by inches. At dawn Sister Amaranth says "Keep doing that," and takes the chair.|You doze between turns and wake on the hour, every hour, as if the ring calls you. The patient wakes clear-eyed. You don't.|You know the charm's weight now and turn it half asleep. At dawn the patient asks your name. You're too tired to remember it.}
> right: Promise next time, and sleep
  health +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {Sister Amaranth turns the charm herself, without comment, and that's worse than a comment.|You go to bed and lie awake a while, counting hours you're not turning.|You leave the cushion on the chair for someone else. You sleep, mostly.}

@event infirmary_curious_case
title: A Curious Case
pool: infirmary_routine
tone: light
art: potion
tags: magic
scene: A student sitting up in an infirmary bed with a strange magical symptom while a stern healer frowns at a thick reference book.
text: {A second-year sits in bed four hiccuping soap bubbles.|In bed four today: a student whose hair has turned to feathers, and keeps trying to migrate.|Bed four has a student who can only speak in rhyme, and is running out of rhymes.} {Sister Amaranth has three books open and no patience left.|Sister Amaranth glances at you. "You. Useful or scarce. Pick."|{season == "winter": Half the ward has the sniffles, and the hiccups have gone round.|Sister Amaranth is writing a stern note to whoever did this.}}
> left: Help look it up
  challenge research apprentice+:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      lore +small
      say: {You find it on page four hundred. A soap-tongue jinx, cured by mint and silence. Sister Amaranth says "Good," without looking up.|You trace the feathers to a half-learned transformation and the cure to a bowl of warm salt water. The student moults gratefully.|You find the rhyming hex and the counter-word. The student's first plain sentence is "thank you", which is a relief to everyone.}
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      lore +tiny
      say: {You read for an hour and find three cures for things nobody has. Sister Amaranth solves it with a cup of tea and a look.|You recommend plucking. Everyone stares at you. Sister Amaranth sends you to fetch towels instead.|You suggest a cure that makes the rhymes worse and slightly operatic. Sister Amaranth fixes it, and you, with a glance.}
> right: Keep the patient company
  charm +small
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You see who can blow the biggest bubble. You lose, but you laugh so hard your ribs ache.|You help the student hold on to the bedframe whenever a flock goes over. Feathers get everywhere, including your lungs.|You rhyme back. You run out of rhymes together, and by then you're both crying with laughter and very sore.}

# ---------------- The Alchemy Laboratory ----------------

@event alchemy_lab_crystal_sill
title: The Crystal on the Sill
pool: alchemy_lab_routine
tone: light
art: gem
tags: magic
scene: A blue crystal growth spilling out of a jar on a laboratory windowsill and creeping slowly down the wall.
text: {On the windowsill sits somebody's abandoned crystal experiment, a jar of blue spikes labelled DO NOT FEED.|The crystal has grown out of its jar and halfway down the wall. The label now reads DO NOT FEED (WE MEAN IT).|The crystal reaches the floor now and has started on the next window. Someone has given it a name and a little hat.} {It chimes faintly when the door slams.|It has grown a new spike toward the warmest cauldron.|{season == "winter": Frost and crystal are racing each other up the glass.|In the afternoon light it throws blue all over the benches.}}
> left: Feed it a pinch of salt
  chance even:
    alchemy +small
    mood +small
    say: {It drinks the salt with a sound like a sigh and grows one perfect new spike. You understand, suddenly, how it's built.|A new spike unfolds slowly, like a fern. You sketch the growth, and it makes sense of a whole chapter.|It grows a spike in your exact direction, which is either affection or aim. You take notes either way.}
  else:
    health -tiny
    mood -tiny
    say: {It shoots out a spike the size of a finger and catches your sleeve and your wrist. The label, you concede, was right.|It grows a spike straight through your notes and into your thumb. You are not the first; there's a list.|It sneezes crystal dust all over you. You sparkle blue until bedtime and itch till morning.}
> right: Snap off a spike for Pell's
  gold +small
  alignment -tiny
  say: {Pell & Daughters pays well for clean crystal, no questions asked. The jar seems to notice the gap.|You snap off a spike while it's busy growing toward the cauldron. It chimes once, sharply, as if taking your name. Pell's pays anyway.|You break off a piece from the bottom where nobody will notice and sell it in the village. The little hat tilts at you when you come back.}

@event alchemy_lab_good_scales
title: The Good Scales
pool: alchemy_lab_routine
tone: light
art: scales
tags: study
scene: A queue of students with jars and papers waiting at a single fine brass balance in a cluttered laboratory, while battered scales sit unused along the bench.
text: {The laboratory has twelve sets of scales and one that's accurate. The queue for it reaches the door.|The queue for the good scales has rules now, chalked on the bench: one weighing each, no hovering, no charms.|The good scales. You can tell from the door how long the wait is by how many people are sighing.} {A third-year at the front is weighing powdered moonstone grain by grain.|The other scales are off by a hair, a whisker or a small mouse.|{season == "summer": It's hot, and every powder in the room is clumping.|The windows have steamed over and nobody can read their numbers.}}
> left: Charm your way to the front
  challenge appeal apprentice:
    win:
      charm +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {You admire the third-year's technique until they wave you ahead out of sheer flattered confusion.|You quote the chalked rules back at the queue in such a reasonable voice that they let you through to weigh "just one thing".|You've learned the queue's weak points. A compliment, a promise and a borrowed spatula, and you're at the scales in a minute.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {The queue turns as one and points at the back. You go to the back.|Someone reads out rule two, NO HOVERING, and looks at you while they do it. The whole queue laughs.|The queue closes ranks like a wall of elbows. You spend the wait at the back, flushed.}
> right: Use the bad scales and correct
  alchemy +small
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You weigh everything twice on two bad scales and average the errors. Your measurements are right; your head hurts.|You work out exactly how wrong the mouse-scale is and correct for it. It's tedious, and the best arithmetic you've done all week.|You've mapped all eleven bad scales' errors on a card in your pocket. It doesn't make the work less dull.}

@event alchemy_lab_mind_my_cauldron
slot: evening
title: Mind My Cauldron?
pool: alchemy_lab_routine
tone: warm
art: cauldron
tags: social
cast:
  mate: any where (tagged(mate, student) or tagged(mate, prefect)) and rel(mate) >= 0 and around(mate)
scene: A student hurrying out of an evening laboratory, pointing back at a bubbling cauldron and an hourglass, while another student is left holding the spoon.
text: {{mate} has a six-hour brew going and a supper {they} can't miss. "Stir it widdershins when the sand runs out. Twice. I owe you."|{mate} spots you across the lab and is already holding out the spoon. "Hourglass. Twice. Widdershins. You're a lifesaver."|{mate} hands you the spoon with the air of someone who knows you'll say yes. The hourglass is half run.} {The brew smells of oranges and thunder.|The brew is the colour of a bruise, and humming.|{season == "winter": The lab windows are black and the cauldrons the only warmth.|Somebody's cauldron at the far end goes off like a cork.}}
> left: Stay and mind it
  alchemy +small
  rel mate +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You miss supper and stir twice, exactly. {mate} comes back to a perfect brew, and you to a rumbling stomach.|You mind it, and pick up three of {mate}'s tricks just by watching the sand. Your own supper is cold bread.|The brew behaves for you. {mate} comes back, sniffs, and says your wrist is better than {theirs}. Your stomach disagrees with the praise.}
> right: Sorry, I've plans
  mood +small
  rel mate -tiny
  say: {You go to supper. {mate} goes to supper late, and the brew turns slightly orange in revenge.|You make your excuses. {mate} finds someone else, and mentions it, twice.|You say no this time. {mate} looks at the spoon, then at you, and sighs the sigh of the betrayed.}

# ---------------- Mir's Workshop ----------------

@event workshop_after_the_bang
title: After the Bang
pool: workshop_routine
tone: light
art: gear
tags: magic
scene: A workshop wreathed in drifting smoke, a soot-blackened inventor waving cheerfully through it, springs and gears scattered across the floor.
text: {You hear the bang from the corridor. Inside, the smoke is thinning, and Mir waves through it, eyebrows mostly intact. "That was the good kind of bang! Probably!"|Another bang. Mir has chalked a tally by the door: GOOD BANGS and BAD BANGS. Today's goes in a third column, MUSICAL.|You don't even flinch at the bang now. Mir hands you a broom before the smoke clears.} {Gears are still rolling across the floor.|A small cog is spinning on its edge, refusing to fall.|{season == "winter": The blast has blown the window open and snow is coming in.|The fire bucket by the door is already empty.}}
> left: Help sweep up and salvage
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You sort springs from gears from bits of the ceiling. A spring bites your finger; Mir says that one's always been like that.|You salvage a box of parts and a burnt sleeve. Mir promises to name the next bang after you.|You sweep like a professional now, and pocket a cog that wouldn't fall. Mir pretends not to see. Your fingers are cut in two places.}
> right: Read the singed blueprint
  arcana +small
  say: {You find the mistake, a rune drawn backwards, and tell Mir, who is delighted, then mortified, then delighted. "Probably that's what it was!"|You study the scorched blueprint while Mir sweeps around you, and learn more from the burn marks than from the lines.|You can read Mir's blueprints now, even singed. You show them where today's bang came from. They want to do it again, on purpose.}

# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger Office ----------------

@event ledger_office_crossword
title: The Crossword Is Late
pool: ledger_office_routine
tone: light
art: paper
tags: club
scene: A half-finished crossword grid pinned above a cluttered desk in a newspaper office, a pencil dangling from a string beside it.
text: {The Ledger's crossword setter has vanished before the deadline, leaving half a grid pinned over the desk and a pencil on a string.|The crossword is late again. The setter's note says "back soon", and has said it since Tuesday.|The crossword grid again, half done. The pencil on its string swings toward you as if it knows.} {Seven across is blank. Nine down is a rude word, crossed out.|A furious letter about last week's puzzle is pinned beside it, in a professor's hand.|{season == "winter": The ink in the pot has a skin of ice.|The press is warming up, and so is everyone's temper.}}
> left: Finish setting it, properly
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You fill the grid with honest clues and one fiendish one. Your eyes ache; it goes to print with your pencil marks still on it.|You set it so carefully that the professor who complained will have nothing to complain about. It takes all evening.|You set the whole grid by lamplight. Nine down is no longer rude. You're a little sorry.}
> right: Hide a message in the clues
  challenge deceive apprentice:
    win:
      guile +small
      renown +small
      say: {The first letters of the across clues spell SOUP IS A CRIME. By lunch the whole refectory has solved it, and your name is going round with it.|You hide a riddle in the down clues. Half the castle spends a week on it, the professor writes in again, delighted, and somebody tells him who set it.|Your hidden message is the talk of breakfast, and the Ledger lets slip who set it.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {Nessa reads the proof, finds SOUP IS A CRIME in the first letters and circles it in red, then circles you.|The message comes out garbled: the clues spell SOUP IS A CRIMP. Nobody understands it. Somehow that's worse.|Nessa spots it at the drying line and pulls the proof. "Clever," she says. "Not printed. Next."}

# ---------------- The Headmistress's Tower ----------------

@event headmistress_tower_window_boxes
title: The Window Boxes
pool: headmistress_tower_routine
tone: warm
art: flower
tags: nature
cast:
  host: isolde
scene: A high tower window open over a dizzying drop to the lake, a row of window boxes of night-flowering herbs along the sill, a calm woman writing at a desk behind.
text: {"Would you see to the window boxes while I finish this?" {host} says, without looking up. The boxes run along the outside of the tower window, high over the lake.|The window boxes again. {host} has left the watering can on the sill, which counts as an invitation.|You reach for the watering can before {host} asks. She smiles at her letter.} {The far box is just out of reach without leaning.|One plant in the far box has opened a single silver flower.|{season == "winter": Frost has nipped the herbs; they need covering.|Bees from somewhere have found the boxes, this high up.}}
> left: Lean out to the far box
  challenge feat apprentice wild:
    win:
      vigor +small
      mood +small
      say: {You lean out over the drop with one hand on the frame and tend the far box. When you pull back in, {host} is watching. "Steady," she says. It's praise.|You reach the far box and the silver flower opens a second bloom as you water it. You stay leaning out a moment longer than you need to, for the view.|You know the reach now: knee on the sill, hand on the frame. The far box is tended in a minute, and the lake glitters far below.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {Your foot slips on the sill and the watering can drops a very long way. {host} doesn't look up. "There's another in the cupboard," she says. Your shin is skinned.|You bang your head on the frame coming back in. {host}'s quill pauses, then goes on. The silver flower doesn't get its water.|You reach, wobble, and decide against it with your heart pounding and an elbow scraped. {host} tends the far box herself afterwards, without comment.}
> right: Tend the near boxes and talk
  lore +small
  say: {You water the near herbs while {host} tells you their names, moonmint, star-sorrel, a thyme that only opens after dark. She knows each one's story.|{host} asks what you're reading this term and listens to the answer as she writes. You leave having said more than you meant to, and learned the names of six plants.|You tend the near boxes while {host} reads you a line from her letter and asks what you'd reply. You think about it on the stairs all the way down.}

@event headmistress_tower_waiting_bench
title: The Waiting Bench
pool: headmistress_tower_routine
tone: light
art: hourglass
tags: social
scene: A narrow stone bench at the foot of a spiral stair, a pale first-year sitting on it clutching a summons, a stone owl watching from above.
text: {On the bench at the foot of the stair sits a first-year with a summons in one hand and doom on their face. "What's she like?" they whisper.|Another summoned first-year on the bench, another whisper: "Is it true she can read minds?"|The waiting bench has a first-year on it again; you suspect it always will. "Have you been in? What happens?"} {The stone owl watches you both.|Somewhere above, a door opens and closes.|{final_year: By next year you'll be gone, and the bench will still have someone on it.|The summons is smudged from being held so hard.}}
> left: Tell them the kind truth
  charm +small
  say: {"She'll listen," you say, "and it will be worse than shouting, and then it will be better." The first-year breathes for the first time in an hour.|"She can't read minds," you say. "She just doesn't need to." The first-year laughs, shakily, and goes up a little taller.|"She'll pour you tea," you say. "Drink it. Tell her the truth." The first-year nods and goes up the stair like someone visiting a friend.}
> right: Tell them a horror story
  guile +small
  mood +small
  alignment -tiny
  say: {You describe the trapdoor, the pit and the crocodiles in loving detail. The first-year goes up grey. You feel wonderful, then less wonderful.|"Only on Thursdays," you say gravely. It's Thursday. The first-year's face is a work of art.|You tell them about the owl that eats liars. The owl, above you, opens one eye and looks at you for a long time.}

# ---------------- The Seventh Tower ----------------

@pool seventh_tower_routine
place: seventh_tower
class: routine
repeat: 5
weight: 10

@event seventh_tower_candle_landing
title: Other People's Candles
pool: seventh_tower_routine
tone: eerie
art: candle
tags: mystery
scene: A narrow stone landing crowded with old candle stubs of every colour, initials scratched into the wall above them, one stub still faintly warm.
text: {Halfway up the seventh tower is a landing crowded with candle stubs, a century of them, with initials scratched in the wall above. Whoever climbed here, climbed with a light.|The candle landing. You recognise some of the initials now, the way you recognise faces in a corridor.|Back at the landing of candles. You've found your own favourite set of initials, deep and careful, and wonder who cut them.} {One stub is still faintly warm.|{season == "winter": The wax is cold as stone.|A draught from nowhere leans every wick the same way.}|One stub has been lit and blown out since your last climb.}
> left: Leave a candle of your own
  gold -tiny
  mood +small
  say: {You light the candle you bought in the village and set it among the others. For a moment the landing is bright, and the initials look almost cheerful.|You light a new candle and sit with it while it burns down. It feels like adding your name to a very long, very quiet register.|You leave a fresh candle beside the careful initials. When you look back from the stair, its flame is leaning toward them.}
> right: Read the initials by lamplight
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You copy down the initials and the dates. Most of them are a century old or more; one is from last year. Nobody you ask admits to it.|You find two sets of initials cut in the same hand, forty years apart. You leave without deciding what that means.|You read the wall until your lamp gutters. All those people, climbing up here with a light, alone. It sits with you all evening.}

@event seventh_tower_wind_song
title: The Wind in the Slits
pool: seventh_tower_routine
tone: eerie
art: music
tags: mystery
scene: Thin arrow-slit windows in the curved wall of a dusty tower stair, wind whistling through them, a student with one hand pressed to the cold stone.
text: {The wind plays the seventh tower's arrow-slits like a flute, a different note at every window.|The tower is playing again. You'd swear the tune has more notes than there are windows.|The wind's song. You can hum the first part now; nobody else in the castle knows it.} {Today it sounds almost like a lullaby.|Today it is quick and fretful, as if late for something.|{season == "winter": It plays low and slow, like something under snow.|It plays in fits, between gusts.}}
> left: Hum along and listen
  mood +small
  mana -small
  say: {You hum back. The tower answers in harmony, and you feel it drawing your magic out through the tune, thin and bright. You come away drained and happy, as after a long song.|You hum, and one window falls silent as if to listen. The tune pulls at your magic like a tide; it costs you something to keep going. You keep going.|You hum the part you know and the tower plays the rest, on your magic as much as the wind. When it's done your fingertips are cold and empty, and you're smiling.}
> right: Write the tune down
  lore +small
  say: {You mark each window's note in your notebook. It's a scale nobody uses any more; you find it later in a very old songbook.|You write the notes down and get a different tune each time you read them back. That seems important, and you can't think why.|Your page of notes is filling up. The tune has the same shape as the stair, you realise; you climb it, more or less.}

@event seventh_tower_outside_ledge
title: The Desks on the Stair
pool: seventh_tower_routine
tone: eerie
art: tower
tags: danger
scene: A narrow tower stair choked with a heap of old wooden classroom desks stacked legs-up, a student with a candle looking for a way through.
text: {Two turns below the top, the stair is choked with old classroom desks, stacked legs-up to the ceiling, the way somebody left them a century ago. There's a gap, just.|The desks on the stair have shifted again; the gap is somewhere else this time.|You know the heap now: the desk with the cracked lid, the one that wobbles, the one you never lean on.} {A draught whistles through the legs.|{season == "winter": Frost has furred the old varnish.|A mouse watches you from an inkwell.}|Your candle finds a carved date, and loses it.}
> left: Shift the desks and clear the way
  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:
    win:
      vigor +small
      mood +small
      say: {You haul the desks off the heap one by one and stand them against the wall, legs down, properly. When you're done the stair is a stair again, and your arms are lead.|You lift, slide and wedge until the gap is wide enough to walk through upright. Someone has been at the heap since; you put it right again.|Cracked lid, wobbler, the one you never lean on: in that order. You're through in ten minutes and up at the top with the dust and the moon.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -small
      say: {The heap shifts the wrong way and a desk comes down the stair after you. You get out of its way, mostly. Your shin does not.|You pull the wrong desk and the whole heap groans. You back down the stair with a splinter the size of a pen in your palm.|The wobbler wobbles. You catch it with your shoulder, hard, and decide the top of the tower can wait.}
> right: Look inside the desks
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You lift one cracked lid. Inside are a quill, a ruler and a page of conjugations copied out a hundred years ago, stopping halfway down. You finish it, for them, and feel oddly sad.|Another lid, another desk: a note passed in class, "meet me by the lake", never answered. Underneath it is the lesson, and you read that instead.|You've been through half the desks now. Every one stops in the middle of something, as if the bell rang and nobody came back.}

# ---------------- The Thesis Laboratory ----------------

@pool thesis_lab_routine
place: thesis_lab
class: routine
repeat: 5
weight: 10

@event thesis_lab_tea_urn
title: The Tea Urn
pool: thesis_lab_routine
tone: warm
art: cup
tags: social study
scene: A great dented copper tea urn on a trolley at the end of a row of study carrels, final-year students clustered round it holding mugs and complaints.
text: {The thesis lab runs on the tea urn, a dented copper monster that has outlived forty years of final-years. Round it, people confess things.|At the urn today someone is crying quietly into their mug about chapter three.|The urn. Someone has taped a list to it: STAGES OF THESIS GRIEF. Everyone has initialled the stage they're at.} {The urn is making the noise that means it's about to boil over.|Someone has hidden a tin of biscuits behind it, labelled NOT BISCUITS.|{season == "spring": The windows are open, and blossom keeps landing in the mugs.|The lamps are lit already, though it's barely evening.}}
> left: Stay and listen
  charm +small
  mood +small
  say: {You hear about an advisor who writes only in question marks, a source that turned out to be a forgery and a cat that ate a bibliography. You say almost nothing, and everyone thanks you.|You sit with the one crying about chapter three until they're laughing about chapter three.|You find your stage on the list and initial it. Then you listen to everyone else's, which is better than any advice.}
> right: Fill your mug and get back to work
  lore +small
  say: {You fill your mug and walk away from the confessions. Your carrel is quiet and your notes go well, and you feel slightly like a deserter.|You pat the crying student's shoulder on your way past and go back to work. It's the best you can do today.|You take your tea back to your carrel. Behind you, the list of stages grows another set of initials.}

@event thesis_lab_old_theses
title: The Shelf of Old Theses
pool: thesis_lab_routine
tone: light
art: book
tags: study
scene: A long shelf of bound theses in faded cloth, a student pulling one out and blowing dust off a spine with a ridiculous title.
text: {Along the back wall run three centuries of finished theses, bound in cloth that has faded to the same brown.|The thesis shelf. You've started a list of the best titles. Today's find: a study of the emotional lives of doorknobs.|The old theses. You can find the good ones by feel now: the spines are worn where people keep taking them down.} {One near the end has a pressed flower for a bookmark.|One has a note on the flyleaf: "Passed. Barely. Worth it."|{season == "spring": Blossom drifts in through the open window and settles on the spines.|A spider has made its home between two famous ones.}}
> left: Read a famous one
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {It's brilliant, and it was written by someone younger than you. You put it back very carefully and go and sit somewhere quiet.|It's the one everybody cites. Reading it in full is like seeing a mountain from the bottom.|You read the most-worn one cover to cover. It's so good it hurts, which you suppose is the point.}
> right: Read a gloriously bad one
  mood +small
  say: {It argues for forty pages that rain is a kind of apology. It passed. You feel enormously better about everything.|You read the doorknob one. It is three hundred pages long and completely serious, and you laugh until the carrels shush you.|You find one with footnotes longer than its chapters, and footnotes to the footnotes. Your own thesis suddenly seems sane.}

@event thesis_lab_mock_defence
title: The Mock Defence
pool: thesis_lab_routine
tone: tense
art: scales
tags: study
scene: A chalkboard at the end of the thesis lab, three final-year students sitting as stern pretend examiners in borrowed gowns while a fourth stands to defend.
text: {Final-years run mock defences at the end of the lab. Three in borrowed gowns play the committee, and they play it cruel.|Mock defence day. The pretend committee has added a fourth chair and a tiny gavel.|The mock committee nods at you as you come in. They've started keeping score on the board.} {Tonight's chief examiner is doing an impression of Thorne that is unkindly accurate.|The chair has a bell, which they ring for every "um".|{season == "winter": Everyone defends in scarves; the lab's fire is out.|Someone has borrowed a skeleton from the infirmary to sit in the audience.}}
> left: Take the hot seat
  challenge research adept-:
    win:
      lore +small
      renown +tiny
      say: {You field every question, even the cruel one about your sources. The chief examiner says "Hm," in Thorne's voice, and means it.|They ring the bell for "um" only twice. That's a record, and the committee applauds with the tiny gavel.|You hold your ground for an hour. Your score on the board is the highest of the term, and people come to ask how.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      lore +tiny
      say: {The third question undoes you. You stand at the board saying "well" while the chief examiner does Thorne's eyebrow at you.|They find the hole in your argument in four minutes, and the bell rings for every "um" after that. Better here than in front of the real thing.|You lose your thread under the gavel. The committee is merciful afterwards, which is worse.}
> right: Sit on the committee
  guile +small
  mood +tiny
  say: {You ask one question, quietly, and watch the defender's face as they see the hole. You feel like a cat. A kind cat.|You learn to find the weak joint in an argument by watching three of them crack. It's educational and a little shameful.|You're the committee's favourite examiner now: gentle voice, terrible questions.}
